    THE SAMPLER'S ERROR CHANGES SIGN BETWEEN THE TWO ARMS.
    With norm_init on it reports slightly MORE distinctness than
    the substrate has; with norm_init off it reports dramatically
    LESS. So it is not a bias that a constant correction removes.

    THAT BREAKS THE STANDING REASSURANCE. The graded-similarity
    note argued that absolute overlaps were suspect but PAIRED
    comparisons survived, since both arms share the engine and the
    load. This pairing is norm_init on vs off -- and it does not
    survive: the sampler's own error is what differs between the
    arms, because norm_init changes which neurons win, hence
    recruitment, hence load, hence the size of the sampler's
    invention. A/B is only safe when the manipulation does not
    move load. norm_init moves it.
